Displacement, strain and rigid motion (@mech-strain-field: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Traction-stress-and-local-force-balance.lingua#r_0YN63QG0NYS92FCEFQBT14BY64", #materials-engineering "Traction-stress-and-local-force-balance.lingua#r_0YN63QG0NYS92FCEFQBT14BY64") { r_4EYCPJSR65WYQKHSN682KNH47F
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Kinematics and Matrix and Index Notation; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), section 2.2 and appendices A–C. Work the notation with two-dimensional examples before using a three-dimensional tensor.

Let u(x) describe the displacement of points from a reference configuration. In small-deformation theory, ε_ij = (∂u_i/∂x_j + ∂u_j/∂x_i)/2. The symmetric gradient describes infinitesimal changes of length and angle. The antisymmetric part describes infinitesimal rotation. Engineering shear γ_xy = 2ε_xy; carrying that factor consistently matters in constitutive matrices and strain-gauge reduction.

For u_x = ax + by and u_y = cx + dy, ε_xx = a, ε_yy = d and γ_xy = b+c. If a=d=0 and b=−c, this field is an infinitesimal rigid rotation with zero small strain. A finite rotation requires the finite-deformation account: F = ∂x_current/∂X_reference and Green–Lagrange strain (FᵀF − 1)/2. A true rigid rotation has FᵀF = 1 and therefore zero finite strain.

**Work:** Transform a small square with extension, shear and rotation separately. Compare actual edge lengths with each predicted strain. Show why using the infinitesimal strain expression for a large rigid rotation can create fictitious strain. Derive the compatible displacement field for a simple assigned strain and explain why arbitrary strain components need not fit into one continuous body.

